Twist Hacking Guide
====================

Copyright (C) 2026 Chris Burdess


Coding style
------------

ISO C99, enforced with -std=c99 -pedantic.  Do not use C11 features
(_Static_assert, <stdatomic.h>, _Alignas, anonymous unions, _Generic).
The reactor's cancel flags and command queue use the __atomic builtins
through src/reactor/atomic.h, which is valid in C99.  Follow the GNU Coding
Standards: GNU indentation, a space before the opening parenthesis of
calls and declarations, return type on its own line in definitions, and
no tabs.  Every public symbol starts with twist_ (types and functions)
or TWIST_ (macros).  The library exports nothing else.

Every source file carries the GPLv3+ notice used in the existing files.
Every translation unit includes config.h first.

Source layout
-------------

libtwist is one library.  src/ holds the public-facing pieces (the
internal header, init, i18n, and the twist command).  The rest is
split by layer, and a new scheme is a new directory:

  src/reactor/   loop, connection, poll, timers, runtime, storage
  src/engine/    URL, easy/multi/share, proxy, scheme table
  src/dns/       DNS client: messages, DoQ/DoT/DoH/UDP order, resolver
  src/auth/      SASL clients, HTTP Basic and Digest, MD5, SHA-256
  src/http/      HTTP/1, HTTP/2, HTTP/3, cookies
  src/ftp/       FTP and FTPS
  src/file/      file://
  src/tls/       quickstep TLS and QUIC under the connection

Private headers stay next to the sources that use them.  Everything
else includes twist-int.h.

Push parsing
------------

Protocol handlers are state machines fed by Endpoint.receive.  The
handler returns how many bytes it consumed.  The connection keeps the
unconsumed tail in one inbound buffer and presents that tail again in
front of the next read.  That buffer is capped at 256 KiB
(TWIST_IN_CEILING).  A socket read asks only for the room left under
the cap, so one readable event cannot allocate past it.

Returning 0 means the tail is an incomplete token and more bytes are
required.  The connection keeps reading until the cap.  If the cap is
full and the handler has not paused, the token does not fit: the
connection fails and the buffer is released.  A missing delimiter
therefore cannot grow memory and cannot pin the cap for the life of
the process.

Backpressure is pause_read and resume_read.  Pause drops read interest,
leaves the buffered tail in place, and lets the TCP window close.
resume_read presents that tail again.  A zero return is not
backpressure.

Parsers do not copy the tail.  A field that must be remembered across
calls is a fixed-size scratch with its own hard maximum (the HTTP/1
line buffer is one).  Bytes copied into that scratch are counted as
consumed so they are not stored twice.  Nothing in the parser allocates
in proportion to the peer's input.  Parsers never block and never read
a socket themselves.  One forward pass.  Bodies are delivered as they
parse; do not buffer a whole message.  Unit tests feed the same input
whole and in small chunks, including 1-byte splits, and retain whatever
the parser did not consume.

What not to put in the loop
---------------------------

The loop, the poll backend and the connection do not mention HTTP.
A new scheme is a twist_scheme row and a ProtocolHandler (or a
datagram handler, or a storage-pool job).  Listen and dial both pin
the endpoint to one loop for life.  Secondary connections of one
transfer (an FTP data socket, another HTTP/3 stream) stay on that loop.

quickstep
---------

TLS and QUIC state machines come from quickstep.  Twist owns the
socket, the monotonic clock and the poll timeout.  gq_tlsauto sits
under the connection so the handler sees plaintext.  A QUIC socket is
one UDP fd on that same loop: recvmmsg, gq_endpoint_recv, gq_endpoint_send
until it yields nothing, sendmmsg.  Each stream is an Endpoint.

Warnings
--------

The tree must build with zero compiler warnings.  Fix the cause; if a
warning is genuinely unavoidable, add a narrow, commented suppression.

Testing
-------

  make check              # unit tests
  make check-integration  # peers on the network
  make distcheck          # before every release

make check runs the programs in tests/, one per subsystem.  They are
deterministic and in-process: no sockets, no files, no wall clock (pass
a virtual clock into the loop).  Anything that dials a peer goes in
integration/ and skips with exit 77 when the peer is absent.
integration/ is not a subdirectory of 'make check'.

The progress meter and bar must match curl's output, since scripts may read
it: the layout and the arithmetic are in tests/test-progress.c and
tests/test-bar.c, with a clock the test sets, and the lines curl wrote for the
same numbers; what the tool shows and
when in tests/cli-progress.sh, on file:// URLs; the sizes over HTTP in
integration/http-progress.sh, against a server of python's.  The environment variables are tested in tests/test-proxyenv.c (which proxy,
and the no-proxy list), tests/test-env.c (the trust store, the key log and
qlog), integration/env.sh (the proxies against a server of python's) and
integration/proxy-https.sh (https proxies, of node's, in front of an origin of
python's; the sftp case is in integration/ssh-openssh.sh; ftps, with a
server of python's that takes the data connection at RETR and starts TLS on it
after its 150 reply, as vsftpd does, is integration/ftps.sh).  HTTP/3, direct and
through a CONNECT-UDP proxy, has unit tests only: tests/test-template.c and
tests/test-masque.c, with the QUIC glue under tests/test-env.c.  No peer for
it is in the tree, since the only one that exists is written in Go.  Tests and
their peers are written in C or Python, the languages the GNU Coding
Standards name.  To
compare with curl, run both on the same URL with COLUMNS set and 2>&1 >/dev/null,
and look at the bytes (| od -c or | cat -v).  A hand test of the tool on a
terminal shows nothing unless the body goes to a file.

Fuzzing
-------

  fuzz/fuzz.sh list                  # the targets
  fuzz/fuzz.sh run -t 60 [-l] [T...] # fuzz each; -l looks for leaks too
  fuzz/fuzz.sh replay T FILE...      # an input that failed, again
  make check-fuzz                    # the inputs that once found a bug

Every parser that takes bytes or text from outside has a harness in fuzz/
(harness-NAME.c), written as for libFuzzer: LLVMFuzzerTestOneInput, and if it
likes, fuzz_seeds and fuzz_tokens.  There is no libFuzzer or AFL on every
machine, so fuzz/engine.c is a small coverage-guided fuzzer that needs only
gcc: the script compiles the library again, in fuzz-build/, with
-fsanitize=address,undefined and -fsanitize-coverage=trace-pc.  With clang a
harness builds with -fsanitize=fuzzer and engine.c is not needed.

A harness frees what it makes, so a leak is a finding.  An input that
fails is written to fuzz-build/artifacts/NAME; when the bug is fixed, put
the input (they are small) in fuzz/regress/NAME/ with a name that says what
it was, write the ordinary test as well, and 'make check-fuzz' will keep it
fixed.  Fixing comes first-test-first as for any bug: a fuzzer finds the
input, the test says what should have happened.

What is fuzzed is what the network or the user can send to twist's own
code: URLs, globs, variables, quote commands, proxy replies, HTTP/1, HTTP/2,
HTTP/3 and the header compressions, DNS messages, content codings, cookies,
Alt-Svc, FTP, SMTP, POP3, IMAP, MQTT, LDAP, TFTP, DICT, Gopher, WebSocket,
SASL and Digest, and the cutting of mail text.  SSH is foxtrot's and the TLS
and QUIC stacks are quickstep's, and are fuzzed there.

Copyright
---------

The project intends to become part of the GNU system with copyright
assigned to the Free Software Foundation.  Until then, keep the notice
"Copyright (C) YEAR Name" in each file, and do not incorporate code
whose licence is not compatible with GPLv3+.

Documentation and translation
-----------------------------

The manual is Texinfo (doc/twist.texi); update it when the public API
or user-visible behaviour changes.  Build it with "make info".
doc/twist.1 is the short man page.

Every string a person might read (twist_strerror, the CLI, diagnostics)
is wrapped in _() and its source file is listed in po/POTFILES.in.  Do
not translate RFC header names or scheme tokens.  A transfer diagnostic
on the CLI has the form "twist: (N) message" on stderr.

Building
--------

  ./bootstrap
  ./configure
  make
  make check

quickstep and foxtrot must already be installed, so that pkg-config can
see quickstep.pc and foxtrot.pc.  foxtrot needs libgcrypt 1.12.4 or later
and configure checks the pkg-config version, the header and the library
the loader gives a program.  zlib is required.  No dependency other than
quickstep, foxtrot (and so libgcrypt), zlib, and GNU readline is added
without explicit approval.  Readline links into the twist program only.
